{"step_index":136,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-28T19:02:09Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nreview and access what is here compared to what is mentioned and report whats here, whats not, and possible changes that may be better, and anything else that any and all agents may reason up that may or may not help.  read only and create the report. go. \"Here's what it must have, beyond the obvious — the things that separate a real product from a demo:\n\nThe differentiators nobody ships:\nSpeculative decoding** — a small draft model predicts tokens, the big model verifies in parallel. Two to three times faster generation, zero quality loss. This is the single biggest \"feels instant\" lever and almost nobody wires it into a desktop agent.\nKV-cache reuse across turns** — don't recompute the system prompt and tool schemas every message. Prefix caching means the second turn is dramatically cheaper. llama.cpp supports this; most wrappers ignore it.\nEmbedding model hot-swappable** — local ONNX MiniLM by default, cloud fallback (OpenAI/Voyage) when you opt in. Same retrieval interface, two backends.\nHybrid retrieval** — BM25 keyword plus dense vector, fused with reciprocal rank. Pure vector search misses exact identifiers; pure keyword misses semantics. You need both.\nReranker pass** — cross-encoder rescoring of the top candidates before they hit context. Cheap, and it measurably lifts groundedness.\nMemory decay + consolidation** — episodic memories fade unless reinforced; a \"sleep\" pass merges related facts into semantic memory. Without this, your blackboard rots into noise.\nCritic agent as a gate** — before any tool mutation commits, a second model pass checks groundedness against retrieved context. Abstain path when confidence is low.\nStreaming tool cards** — each tool call renders as a live card in the side panel: status, args, result, duration. Cursor does this; you should too, but with richer detail.\nFull agent roster access** — every agent, every session, every memory, browsable and searchable in one pane. Cross-agent context sharing with permission boundaries.\nCloud optional fallback** — local-first, but if the local model is slow or unavailable, seamlessly route to a configured cloud endpoint. User never notices the handoff unless they look.\nAccelerators** — GPU offload auto-detect, CPU thread pinning, speculative decoding toggle, context window budgeting. All exposed in settings, all with sane defaults.\n\nThe Cursor-parity surface:\nSide panel with Agent / Ask / Plan / Debug modes (Shift+Tab cycles)\nCommand palette (Ctrl+Shift+P)\nInline edit (Ctrl+K)\nModel cycling (Ctrl+/)\nVoice mode toggle (Ctrl+Shift+Space)\nSettings: models, rules, indexing, memory, accelerators, cloud fallback\nFile tree, diff viewer, terminal integration\n\nThe stack, no compromise:\nZig core — agent loop, memory engine, retrieval, HTTP server, all in one process. No Python runtime, no Node, no daemon.\nC/C++ only where it earns its place: llama.cpp for inference, sqlite3 for the blackboard (FTS5 + vector extension), a small ONNX runtime for embeddings.\nWebView frontend — single HTML/CSS/JS bundle, no framework bloat. Reactive via a tiny custom signal system.\nOne binary, one database file, zero external services required.\"\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-28T15:02:09-04:00.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
